   "source": [
    "**Interpretation.** Median net wealth rises steeply with age, from about DKK 69,000 for 18–29-year-olds to a peak of about DKK 2.17 million for 60–69-year-olds, and then falls for older groups. P90 follows the same hump shape, so the spread within each age group grows with age: the widest upper gap (P90 − P50) is in the 60–69 group at about **DKK 4.75 million**, closely followed by the 70–79 group (DKK 4.70 million). The lower gap (P50 − P10) is much smaller in every group, so inequality within an age group is mainly driven by the top.\n",
    "\n",
    "P10 is negative for the **18–29 and 30–39** groups (DKK −14,000 and −118,000). Young adults are more likely to have more debt than assets because they borrow against their future income: they take out student loans and buy their first home with a large mortgage while their down payment and housing equity are still small, and they have not yet had time to save or build up pension wealth. This matches the life-cycle model, where people borrow when young, save during their working life and draw down their wealth in retirement.\n",
    "\n",
    "Note that the data are one cross-section from 2024: we compare different people of different ages, not the same people as they age, so the profiles also reflect differences between cohorts (for example in house price gains and pension schemes)."
   ]
  },
