    "**Interpretation.** The three brackets below zero are \"under −1,000,000 DKK\", \"−1,000,000 to −500,000 DKK\" and \"−500,000 to 0 DKK\". The share of adults with negative net wealth is highest among 30–39-year-olds (**15.6%**), followed by 18–29-year-olds (12.2%). It then falls steadily to about 3% from age 60 and to 1.9% for those aged 90 and over. The eight age counts sum to 4,851,818, which equals the published all-adult count.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Contrast with the median profile.** The median peaks late, at ages 60–69, while the share with negative wealth peaks early, at ages 30–39. The 30–39 group has a clearly positive median (about DKK 370,000) but also the largest share in debt. Many people in their thirties have just bought a home with a large mortgage and little equity, so a small fall in house prices or a high loan-to-value ratio leaves them below zero, while the typical person in the group already owns more than they owe. Many 18–29-year-olds have not yet taken on a mortgage, so fewer of them are in debt even though their median is much lower. After 60 the median falls as people draw down their wealth in retirement, but the share with negative wealth stays flat at about 3%: older people spend out of positive wealth rather than going into debt."
