   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "9e7c1906",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "**Interpretation.** The age shares are each age count divided by the total of 4,851,818 adults, and they sum to one. Each simulated adult draws u from the uniform distribution on [0, 1) and is placed in the age group whose interval contains u, so the probability of belonging to a group equals its share. Each adult is then given the median (P50) wealth of their age group.\n",
    "\n",
    "The simulated shares differ slightly from the published ones, by at most about 0.5 percentage points, because the age groups are random draws: with 10,000 adults the share in each group varies by chance around its true probability (sampling error). The standard error of a share p is √(p(1−p)/N), about 0.4 percentage points for p = 0.2 and N = 10,000, so differences of this size are expected. They would shrink as N grows (law of large numbers), and with the same seed we get exactly the same draws every time."
   ]
