   "source": [
    "# a. summary statistics for a wealth distribution\n",
    "def summary(wealth):\n",
    "    p10, p50, p90 = np.percentile(wealth, [10, 50, 90])\n",
    "    return {'P10': p10, 'P50': p50, 'P90': p90, 'P90 - P10': p90 - p10,\n",
    "            'negative wealth, %': 100*np.mean(wealth < 0)}\n",
    "\n",
    "# b. observed Denmark from the all-adult rows\n",
    "obs = percentiles_all.set_index('PERCENTIL')['value']\n",
    "neg_all = counts_all.loc[counts_all['FORMUE'].isin(negative_brackets), 'value'].sum()\n",
    "denmark = {'P10': obs['P10'], 'P50': obs['P50'], 'P90': obs['P90'],\n",
    "           'P90 - P10': obs['P90'] - obs['P10'],\n",
    "           'negative wealth, %': 100*neg_all/counts_all['value'].sum()}\n",
    "\n",
    "# c. one table with three rows\n",
    "results = pd.DataFrame([denmark, summary(wealth_median), summary(wealth_var)],\n",
    "                       index=['Observed Denmark', 'Age-group medians only', 'Variation within age groups'])\n",
    "display(results.style.format({'P10': '{:,.0f}', 'P50': '{:,.0f}', 'P90': '{:,.0f}',\n",
    "                              'P90 - P10': '{:,.0f}', 'negative wealth, %': '{:.1f}'}))\n",
    "\n",
    "# d. fraction of the observed gap reproduced by the first economy\n",
    "share_gap = results.loc['Age-group medians only', 'P90 - P10'] / results.loc['Observed Denmark', 'P90 - P10']\n",
    "print(f'Medians-only economy reproduces {100*share_gap:.1f}% of the observed P90 - P10 gap')"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "52240b43",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "**Interpretation.**\n",
    "\n",
    "**1. Age-group medians only.** This economy reproduces only **46.7%** of the observed P90 − P10 gap (DKK 2.10 million against 4.50 million). So less than half of the wealth gap between the 10th and 90th percentile can be explained by differences between age groups. The rest comes from differences between people of the same age. P10 and P90 in this economy are simply the medians of the poorest age group (18–29: DKK 68,806) and the richest (60–69: DKK 2,166,898). It contains no one with negative wealth because every adult gets the median of their age group, and all eight medians are positive. Even among 30–39-year-olds, where 15.6% are in debt, the typical person owns more than they owe.\n",
    "\n",
    "**2. Variation within age groups.** Because this economy draws directly from each age group's published percentiles, it should come very close to Denmark: if each age group's wealth is reproduced, the mixture of age groups reproduces the national distribution. Indeed the median is almost identical (DKK 813,339 vs. 813,665), the gap is 97% of the observed gap, and 7.5% have negative wealth compared with 8.0%. A difference remains for several reasons:\n",
    "- **Sampling error**: with 10,000 adults, both the age groups and the ranks are random draws, so the simulated shares and percentiles differ by chance from the population (compare the simulated age shares in 2.1).\n",
    "- **Cut tails and discrete ranks**: each adult gets a percentile *threshold* (P1, …, P99), not a value drawn from the full distribution. No one gets wealth below P1 or above P99, so the extreme debt and the very richest are missing, and everybody between two thresholds is given the same value. This compresses the tails, which is why P90 and the share with negative wealth are slightly too low."
   ]
  },
