   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "65ffee72",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "**Interpretation.**\n",
    "\n",
    "**1. Effects of an older population.**\n",
    "- **Higher median**: the median rises by about DKK 131,000, from 813,000 to 945,000. The age profiles in Part 1 show that 18–29-year-olds have the lowest median of all groups (DKK 69,000), while 70–79-year-olds have the third-highest (DKK 1.73 million). Moving people from the poorest to one of the richest age groups pushes the whole distribution up.\n",
    "- **Wider P90 − P10 gap**: the gap widens by about DKK 275,000, to DKK 4.64 million. The 70–79 group has a very wide distribution (P90 of DKK 6.4 million and an upper gap of DKK 4.7 million), while the 18–29 group is concentrated at low values (P90 of only DKK 0.54 million). More people in an age group with a wide spread, and in particular a high top, raise P90 much more than P10, so the gap widens.\n",
    "- **Fewer people with negative wealth**: the share falls from 7.5% to 7.1%. In Part 1, 12.2% of 18–29-year-olds have negative net wealth compared with only 3.0% of 70–79-year-olds, so moving 5% of the population from the first group to the second removes many of those in debt.\n",
    "\n",
    "**2. Same seed.** With the same seed, both runs use exactly the same random numbers u and the same ranks. The only thing that changes is the age shares, so the difference between the two runs measures the effect of the older population and not random noise from new draws (common random numbers). With a different seed, part of the change could be sampling error of the same size as the differences we are trying to measure.\n",
    "\n",
    "**3. Fixed age profiles.** The experiment assumes that each age group keeps its 2024 wealth distribution. If the population really aged, the profiles would likely change. For example, more older people selling their homes and drawing down savings could lower house and asset prices, which would reduce the wealth of older groups. A larger pension-age population could also lead to higher taxes or lower public pensions, so that people save more (or less) during their working life. Finally, the profiles also reflect cohort effects: today's 70–79-year-olds benefited from large house price gains, and future old cohorts with different pension schemes and housing histories will not have the same wealth."
   ]
